    "content": "Passover (Pesach) is a major 7-8 day Jewish holiday beginning on 15 Nisan (April 1-9, 2026) that commemorates the biblical Exodus, where Israelites were freed from Egyptian slavery. Key traditions include holding a Seder, eating matzah, and removing all leavened bread (chametz) from homes to honor the hurried escape. Story and History The Exodus: Occurring around 3,400 years ago, the story details how Moses demanded the release of the Israelites from Pharaoh. After refusing, Egypt was struck by ten plagues. The \"Passing Over\": The final plague involved the death of the firstborn male, but God \"passed over\" the homes of the Israelites, who had marked their doorposts with lamb's blood, sparing them. Freedom: Following this, Pharaoh allowed the Israelites to leave, but they left in such a hurry that their bread did not have time to rise, leading to the tradition of eating unleavened bread (matzah). Significance of the Passover SederThe Seder is a 15-step ritual-rich feast that serves as the central experience of Passover, acting as a \"master story\" for the Jewish people. Telling the Story: The primary purpose is to pass the history of the Exodus down to new generations, often facilitated by the Haggadah. Symbolic Foods: Participants eat specific foods, such as maror (bitter herbs for the bitterness of slavery) and matzah (unleavened bread). Four Cups of Wine: Four cups of wine are consumed to represent the promises of redemption. Celebration and Traditions Cleaning: Before the holiday, homes are thoroughly cleaned to remove all chametz (leavened grain products). Prohibition of Leaven: For the duration, Jews avoid eating bread, pasta, and cookies, often selling their remaining chametz to non-Jews. Community: The holiday focuses on family, community, and solidarity, often including guests at the Seder table. Passover Around the World Duration: In Israel, it is celebrated for seven days, while many Diaspora communities celebrate for eight days. Customs: While the core, biblical requirements (eating matzah, telling the story) are universal, traditions can vary, such as whether to eat legumes (kitniyot), which Ashkenazi Jews traditionally avoided but some have recently begun to incorporate. 2026-2030 Dates 2026: Sunset, April 1 – Nightfall, April 9. 2027: Sunset, April 21 – Nightfall, April 29. 2028: Sunset, April 10 – Nightfall, April 18. 2029: Sunset, March 30 – Nightfall, April 7. 2030: Sunset, April 17 – Nightfall, April 25.",
